      "title": "feat(govern): redact values assigned to credential-like names in text",
      "body": "## What does this MR do and why?\n\n`glab govern audit sync` only redacts a value by its name when the name is a JSON key. A secret in an unknown format is sent as it is in a command such as `export MY_API_TOKEN=...`, or in a `.env` or YAML file the agent writes.\n\nThis applies the same credential names (`sensitiveKey`) to `NAME=value` and `NAME: value` in text, and replaces the value with `[REDACTED:credential]`.\n\n- The pattern only matches the name and its separator. The value is read by `assignedValue`, so a name inside another value is still found, for example `args: --token=...`, `$env:DB_PASSWORD = \"...\"` or `url=https://h/p?token=...`.\n- A quoted value runs to its closing quote, including escaped quotes such as `\\\"...\\\"`. Any other value ends at a space, quote, backslash or separator (`& , ; | ) ] }`).\n- To avoid false positives, the value must have a digit and must not start with `$`, `<`, `{`, `[`, `&`, `(`, `=`, `/` or `~`. This keeps type annotations (`token: Optional[str]`, `apiKey: ApiKeyType`), comparisons (`password == ...`), calls, paths (`PWD=/home/...`), variables, placeholders and prose such as `password: see 1Password`.\n- Values are read for at most 256 characters, and only the start of a longer value is replaced. Redaction stays linear: about 1 MB of adversarial input (chained `a=`, `next_token=`, quoted and escaped values) takes 60 to 200 ms.\n\nKnown limits, as with the rest of the redaction this is best effort:\n\n- A value without a digit is kept, such as `password: correcthorsebattery`.\n- An unquoted value is only redacted up to a `,`, `;` or `&` in it.\n- Some common names, such as `OPENAI_API_KEY` and `PGPASSWORD`, aren't in the credential name list yet. That's tracked separately in #8728.\n\n## How to validate\n\n```shell\ngo test ./internal/commands/govern/audit/sync/ -run TestRedact\n```\n\nThe new cases in `TestRedactSecrets` cover the redacted values and the false positives above.\n\nCloses #8695",
